      "obligation": "refuter:4:1",
      "id": "algebraic-zariski-main-for-quasi-finite-morphisms",
      "location": "Overview paragraph 3, beginning “The main theorem is the local form...”",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The page says “the local form ... [is] choice-free.” But the in-scope local theorem states “Assume the Axiom of Choice,” and its proof step 8.1 says AC is used through [L6], the nowhere-quasi-finiteness lemma. That lemma also assumes AC and invokes going down, lying over, and minimal-prime descent. The page's choice-free description of the local form contradicts the proof provided.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "c081303fc13711061dc1b1d08f7b41983925f7c82936b52bf9d7fc9fd0b24d56"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:4:2",
      "id": "cor-affine-normalization-is-finite",
      "location": "4.2",
      "defect": "ill-formed",
      "evidence": "The cited dependency `thm-rational-maps-to-affine-variety-function-field` states that maps X ⇢ Y correspond to injective homomorphisms k(Y) → k(X). Thus the inverse isomorphism k(Y) → k(X) corresponds to a map X ⇢ Y, not the stated θ:Y ⇢ X. With ν:Y → X, the written compositions θ∘ν and ν∘θ are not type-correct. The preceding field-isomorphism argument still establishes birationality; reversing θ's direction repairs this auxiliary sentence.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "5b7771d81543e7ac30ebc190d25ec263de631656b0f7f631b8b881a75c09ef65"
    }
